The Jungle Room in Graceland is one of the most famous rooms in Elvis Presley's home and now a popular tourist attraction. It is located in Memphis, Tennessee, at the Graceland estate, which has been turned into a museum dedicated to the life and legacy of Elvis Presley.\cb1 \
\cb3 The Jungle Room is an informal living area, also known as the den. The room's design and decor evoke a Polynesian or tropical theme, giving it a unique and exotic atmosphere. It features a waterfall made of stone, shag carpeting on both the floor and the ceiling, an abundance of greenery, and dark wooden furniture with animal-like carvings. The room gets its name from the jungle-like ambiance created by these design elements.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Elvis used the Jungle Room as a place to relax, entertain friends and family, and sometimes, to hold informal recording sessions. In fact, some of his later recordings were made in the Jungle Room, which had been temporarily transformed into a makeshift recording studio.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The Jungle Room in Graceland features several interesting and unique objects that contribute to its exotic ambiance:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Waterfall: One of the most notable features in the Jungle Room is the indoor waterfall, which covers an entire wall. The waterfall is made from stone and adds a soothing background sound, contributing to the room's jungle-like atmosphere.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Furniture: The Jungle Room is furnished with custom-made pieces from the late 1960s and early 1970s, many of which were carved from dark wood and had animal-like designs. The furniture includes a large wooden chair shaped like a hand, a wooden tiki bar, and a hexagonal coffee table.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Carpeting: The Jungle Room is known for its green shag carpeting, which not only covers the floor but also the ceiling. This unusual design choice adds to the room's distinctive and cozy atmosphere.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Greenery: The Jungle Room features a variety of plants, both real and artificial, that create a lush, tropical ambiance. The plants are placed throughout the room, in pots and hanging baskets, contributing to the room's jungle-like feel.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Recording equipment: In 1976, the Jungle Room was temporarily converted into a recording studio, and Elvis recorded parts of his albums "From Elvis Presley Boulevard, Memphis, Tennessee" and "Moody Blue" there. The room was equipped with recording equipment, including microphones, cables, and soundproofing materials.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Lighting: The room's lighting includes several distinctive fixtures, such as a tiki-style chandelier and table lamps with animal motifs, which enhance the exotic atmosphere of the Jungle Room.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 These objects, along with other decorative elements, create the unique and memorable atmosphere that makes the Jungle Room one of Graceland's most iconic and intriguing spaces.}